    "---\n",
    "## Task 8 — Report / Blog <a id='9'></a>\n",
    "\n",
    "### 📝 Fine-Tuning DistilBERT for POS Tagging & Chunking: A Practical Deep-Dive\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "#### 🔍 What Are POS Tagging and Chunking?\n",
    "\n",
    "**Part-of-Speech (POS) Tagging** labels each word in a sentence with its grammatical role — noun (NN), verb (VBZ), preposition (IN), proper noun (NNP), and so on. It operates at the *word level* and produces a flat label per token. POS tags are foundational to virtually every NLP pipeline: parsing, machine translation, information extraction, and question answering all rely on them.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Chunking** (shallow parsing / phrase detection) goes one layer deeper. Instead of labelling individual words, it groups consecutive words into *syntactic phrases* — noun phrases (NP), verb phrases (VP), prepositional phrases (PP), etc. Chunking uses the **BIO tagging scheme**:\n",
    "- `B-NP` — beginning of a noun phrase\n",
    "- `I-NP` — inside (continuation) of a noun phrase\n",
    "- `O`    — outside any phrase\n",
    "\n",
    "**Example:**\n",
    "```\n",
    "Sentence:  John  works  at  Google  in  California  .\n",
    "\n",
    "POS:       NNP   VBZ    IN  NNP     IN  NNP         .\n",
    "\n",
    "Chunks:    [John]NP  [works]VP  [at Google]PP  [in California]PP  .\n",
    "BIO:       B-NP      B-VP       B-PP  I-PP      B-PP  I-PP        O\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "#### 🤖 Why Transformers for Token Classification?\n",
    "\n",
    "Traditional sequence labelling (HMMs, CRFs, BiLSTMs) dominated NLP for decades. Transformers transformed this landscape by offering:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Bidirectional context**: BERT reads the entire sentence at once via self-attention. Every word can attend to every other word simultaneously — critical for resolving ambiguous labels like \"bank\" (financial institution vs. river bank).\n",
    "2. **Pre-trained representations**: Hundreds of millions of parameters encode rich syntactic and semantic knowledge before any fine-tuning.\n",
    "3. **Transfer efficiency**: Fine-tuning on 14,000 CoNLL sentences achieves results that previously required far larger annotated corpora.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why DistilBERT?** DistilBERT uses knowledge distillation to compress BERT-base to 66M parameters (vs. 110M) with only ~3% performance loss. Training is roughly 60% faster with 40% less memory — ideal for internship-scale experiments.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "#### ⚙️ Challenges Faced\n",
    "\n",
    "**1. Subword-Label Alignment**  \n",
    "The most technically involved step. WordPiece splits words into subword units: \"running\" → `[\"run\", \"##ning\"]`. Word-level labels must be expanded to match. The solution: assign the word's label to the *first* subword and `-100` to all continuations, excluding them from cross-entropy loss. Getting this logic exactly right — using `tokenizer.word_ids()` rather than character offsets — is essential for correct training.\n",
    "\n",
    "**2. BIO Structural Constraints for Chunking**  \n",
    "The model must learn that `B-NP` must precede `I-NP` — outputting `I-NP` without a prior `B-NP` is structurally invalid. Standard cross-entropy loss does not enforce this. In production, a CRF decoding layer on top of DistilBERT would solve this elegantly.\n",
    "\n",
    "**3. Class Imbalance**  \n",
    "Frequent labels (NN, NNP, I-NP) dominate both datasets. Rare labels (B-INTJ, B-LST) appear only dozens of times. This can suppress rare-class F1. Balanced sampling or class-weighted loss functions are potential remedies.\n",
    "\n",
    "**4. Evaluation Metric Strictness**  \n",
    "`seqeval` counts a phrase as correct only if *every* token in the span has the right label. A single wrong token anywhere invalidates the entire phrase. This is why seqeval F1 is typically lower than token-level accuracy — and also why it is the appropriate metric for NLP sequence labelling.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "#### 📊 Observations & Insights\n",
    "\n",
    "| Observation | Detail |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| POS > Chunking (F1) | POS tagging is inherently easier — flat independent labels, no span coherence required |\n",
    "| DistilBERT is competitive | 40% fewer params than BERT-base; achieves ~97% of BERT-base performance |\n",
    "| BIO boundary errors | The model occasionally predicts `I-NP` at the start of a phrase — CRF would fix this |\n",
    "| First 2 epochs dominate | Most F1 gain occurs in epochs 1–2; epoch 3 gives marginal improvement |\n",
    "| `seqeval` vs accuracy | Token accuracy is higher than seqeval F1 — span evaluation is the meaningful metric |\n",
    "| Rare labels suffer | B-CONJP, B-INTJ, B-LST show lower F1 due to very few training examples |\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "#### 🚀 Recommendations\n",
    "\n",
    "- **CRF decoding layer**: Enforce valid BIO transitions at inference time.\n",
    "- **RoBERTa / DeBERTa**: Better pre-training objectives → higher F1 on CoNLL benchmarks.\n",
    "- **Longer sequences**: Increase `MAX_LEN` to 256 for long sentences with complex phrase structures.\n",
    "- **Cross-lingual transfer**: `xlm-roberta-base` can tag 100+ languages from a single fine-tuned model.\n",
    "- **Larger training data**: Full CoNLL-2003 with data augmentation (back-translation) improves rare-class recall.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "*Report prepared as part of Data Science Internship – February 2026*"
   ]
